<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saône</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saône is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and flowing about 470 km south to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>. Known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as the Arar, it was a major trade route linking the Rhône valley with northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated part of the migrating Helvetii at a crossing of the Arar in 58 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saone.jpg" alt="Saône" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyon_la_Sa%C3%B4ne_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
